<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
    <!--
        The one thing every control in this palette has to do: take its
        Foreground and Background from whatever is hosting it.

        This cannot be left to inheritance, and that is the whole reason this
        file exists. WPF resolves an unset Foreground against the control's
        THEME style before it considers inheritance, and the built-in theme
        style for Button, CheckBox, TextBox and friends sets Foreground itself.
        So a control with no Foreground setter does not inherit the pane's
        colour; it lands on the system default and comes out near-black on a
        dark Office theme, while the TextBlock beside it - not a Control, so no
        theme style - inherits correctly and looks fine. That mismatch is
        exactly what "some text changes colour and a lot does not" looks like.

        Binding to one ancestor is not enough either. A task pane has a
        UserControl to bind to; a plain Window does not, and the binding
        silently yields nothing. PriorityBinding takes the first that resolves,
        so the same style works in both hosts.

        Every Control-derived style in this folder derives from this one.
        TextBlock, Path, Separator and Hyperlink do not: they are not Controls,
        they have no theme style competing with inheritance, and they are
        better left to inherit.
    -->

    <Style x:Key="ThemedControl" TargetType="Control">
        <Setter Property="Foreground">
            <Setter.Value>
                <PriorityBinding>
                    <Binding Path="Foreground" RelativeSource="{RelativeSource AncestorType=UserControl}"/>
                    <Binding Path="Foreground" RelativeSource="{RelativeSource AncestorType=Window}"/>
                </PriorityBinding>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

</ResourceDictionary>
